          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Lorck directly presents his love for Constance as true and emphasizes both its seriousness and its unexpectedly great extent. Avowal is the expression's clearest and dominant mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal immediately grounds an offer of Lorck's whole self, life and soul, followed by a request that Constance accept it. Although the explicit self-giving and request are distinct clauses, they form one integrated turn and position the focal declaration as an affirmation and enactment of offered devotion rather than as a merely descriptive report. The commitment is rhetorically sweeping but its practical terms are unspecified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"nNej, uf nej,* sa hun, og veg uvilkaarlig\\ntilbage.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Despite the emotionally charged confrontation, the focal words are delivered as a deliberate avowal, elaborated with a claim of seriousness and an appeal for acceptance. The supplied passage does not represent this occurrence as a reflex-like discharge beyond its assertive and undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"The occurrence's operative functions are adequately captured by avowal and the contextually integrated offer of devotion. Its appeal to Constance does not establish a further core mode distinct from T and P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During an increasingly insistent declaration of passion, Lorck addresses Constance after she has objected and asked him to stop. He repeats that he loves her, insists that it is serious, and offers her himself, his life and his soul. Constance immediately refuses and retreats; the interaction then becomes openly hostile and coercive.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The same turn offers total personal devotion—“Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl”—and asks Constance to accept it. This contextual clause is grammatically distinct from “Jeg elsker dig,” but it is not an unrelated nearby promise: it supplies the avowal's immediate relational and undertaking force. The offered commitment is figurative and lacks concrete terms. Constance explicitly rejects it with “Nej, uf nej” and moves away, so there is no acceptance or mutual undertaking.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The characters and language are recognisable as a passage from Amalie Skram's novel “Constance Ring”; the classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Lorck as passionately affected through his words, trembling voice and nervous behaviour, but it cannot conclusively establish the sincerity, durability or precise practical content of his professed love and self-offering.\"}"
